Well, it could also be that the DevTeam only recently found out what
most programmers know:  Without a deadline, a release is unlikely to
actually happen.  If someone wants to remedy this situation, give the
DevTeam a reasonable release deadline (e.g. half a year from now) and
the names of a bunch of people who are willing to take over the task of
maintaining an "official" version afterwards; into a similar direction,
but with a well-defined release cycle.

Not that I think that a release would actually happen within the
deadline, the DevTeam working as they are.  But, even if it doesn't help
them _and_ they are unwilling to yield control of the development
source, it would make a fork from 3.4.3 by that group after that
deadline more legitimate than the current unofficial forks.  At least
for those who need an official variant, such as NAO or DevNull.

Just a thought – not entirely earnest.

